## Why This Is Asked
Tests a 3D DP on a grid with an exact count constraint and path reconstruction, a realistic routing/optimization pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP on (i,j,t) with 3 dimensions
- Exact-count handling via -inf initialization
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space optimizations via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithBoosts(grid, boost, K){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const neg = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, () => Array.from({length:m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(neg)));
  const par = Array.from({length:n}, () => Array.from({length:m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  const b00 = boost[0][0] ? 1 : 0;
  dp[0][0][b00] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for (let j=0;j<m;j++){
      for (let t=0;t<=K;t++){
        const add = boost[i][j] ? 1 : 0;
        if (i===0 && j===0) continue;
        const candFromTop = i>0 ? dp[i-1][j][t - add] : neg;
        const candFromLeft = j>0 ? dp[i][j-1][t - add] : neg;
        const bestPrev = Math.max(candFromTop, candFromLeft);
        if (bestPrev > neg/2) {
          dp[i][j][t] = bestPrev + grid[i][j];
          par[i][j][t] = candFromTop >= candFromLeft ? ['top'] : ['left'];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n-1][m-1][K], path: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the actual path coordinates efficiently?
- How would you optimize memory to O(m*K) and still reconstruct the path?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 2D DP with adjacency constraints and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- dp[i][t] for max sum with i elements and t picks
- Transitions handling adjacent restriction
- Base cases and backtracking for indices
- Space optimization via rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonAdjSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  dp[0][1] = A[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    dp[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const skip = dp[i-1][t];
      const take = (i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : -Infinity)) + A[i];
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction can store choices in a separate table
  return {sum: dp[n-1][k], indices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reconstruct the exact indices efficiently? 
- How would you optimize to O(k) space with correct backtracking?

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: exact k segments, per-segment cost, and sum-based values, requiring both interval DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- DP over i (prefix length) and t (blocks)
- Reconstructing endpoints from choice pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(A,k,c){
  const n=A.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(const x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const pos=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=(P[i]-P[0])-c, pos[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let s=t-1;s<=i-1;s++){
        const val=dp[s][t-1]+(P[i]-P[s])-c;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; pos[i][t]=s; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i=n, t=k;
  const blocks=[];
  while(t>0){
    const s=pos[i][t];
    blocks.push([s, i-1]);
    i=s; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This gauges ability to cast a line-wrapping task into a clear 1D DP with a non-linear line cost and a reconstruction path. It tests handling of last-line quirks and feasibility checks.

## Key Concepts
- 1D DP over word indices; dp[n] = 0 as base.
- Prefix sums to compute line width in O(1) per extension.
- Cost function uses squared unused space; last line excluded.
- Reconstruct solution via next indices.
- Time: O(n^2); Space: O(n).

## Code Example
```javascript
function minWrap(words, W) {
  const n = words.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + words[i];
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  const nxt = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  dp[n] = 0;
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      const width = (pref[j + 1] - pref[i]) + (j - i);
      if (width <= W) {
        const cost = (W - width) * (W - width) + dp[j + 1];
        if (cost < dp[i]) { dp[i] = cost; nxt[i] = j + 1; }
      } else break;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { minPenalty: dp[0], next: nxt };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to penalize the last line as well?
- How would you incorporate tie-breaking to prefer fewer lines?

