## Why This Is Asked
This tests deriving a clean DP where subarray cost depends on endpoints, not internal aggregates, and requires careful index handling and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP state dp[i][t] for first i elements into t blocks
- Transition over split point p with endpoint cost |A[p+1]-A[i]| 
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints
- Time O(n^2 k), space O(nk) (can optimize with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minEndPointPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = Math.abs(A[0] - A[i]);
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + Math.abs(A[p + 1] - A[i]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let endpoints = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = par[i][t];
    endpoints.unshift([p + 1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { cost: dp[n - 1][k], endpoints };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if cost were |A[l]-A[r]|^2 or involved a different function?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue tricks if the cost has structure?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP on strings, palindrome precomputation, and reconstruction. It also requires handling a non-trivial scoring function beyond simple lengths.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome table isPal[i][j] for substring s[i..j]
- dist[i][j] stores number of distinct characters in s[i..j]
- DP state dp[i][t]: best score for first i chars partitioned into t palindromes
- Transition: for all j < i with isPal[j][i-1], dp[i][t] = max(dp[i][t], dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1])
- Reconstruction via parent pointers to recover cut positions
- Time/space: O(n^2 k) time and O(n^2 k) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalPartitionScore(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { isPal[i][i] = true; dist[i][i] = 1; }
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j] && (len === 2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j] = true;
      const seen = new Set();
      for (let p = i; p <= j; p++) seen.add(s[p]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        if (isPal[j][i-1] && dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
          const cand = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = j; }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    cuts.push([j, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return { score: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize memory with rolling DP when n is large?
- How does the approach adapt if the score uses a different function, e.g., length of palindrome minus distinct count?
- What are the practical limits for n and k for this to run within time constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a nontrivial DP for partitioning with a per-block cost derived from a range statistic, plus reconstruction across k blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute cost[l][r] = max(A[l..r])*(r-l+1) in O(n^2).
- DP: dp[i][t] = max_{l≤i} dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i].
- Track par[i][t] for block endpoints; base dp[i][1] = cost[0][i].
- Space/time tradeoffs; discuss optimizations (rolling arrays, divide-and-conquer). 

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, a[r]);
      cost[l][r] = m * (r - l + 1);
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a faster optimization (e.g., divide-and-conquer) if opt indices are monotone?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks or variable block values (e.g., using different per-block metrics)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over time with multiple constraints: multiple transactions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Two DP states: cash (not holding) and hold (holding).
- Transitions model buy/sell with transaction count t.
- Base cases initialize day 0; backtracking recovers trade days.
- Space optimization possible to O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfit(prices, k){
  const n = prices.length;
  if (n===0 || k===0) return 0;
  const cash = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const hold = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) cash[0][t] = (t===0)?0:-1e15;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++){
      cash[i][t] = cash[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) cash[i][t] = Math.max(cash[i][t], hold[i-1][t] + prices[i-1]);
      hold[i][t] = hold[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i][t], cash[i-1][t-1] - prices[i-1]);
    }
  }
  let best = 0;
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) best = Math.max(best, cash[n][t]);
  return best;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the exact trade days from the DP table?
- How does the solution change if k >= n/2 (unlimited transactions)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft DP with a left-anchored subarray value and exact block counts, plus reconstruction for endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exactly k blocks
- Precomputation of bestStartAt[l][i]
- Reconstructing block boundaries via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeBestStartDP(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const bestStart = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(-Infinity));
  for (let l=0; l<n; l++) {
    let best = -Infinity;
    for (let i=l; i<n; i++) {
      const sum = pref[i+1]-pref[l];
      best = Math.max(best, sum);
      bestStart[l][i] = best;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) dp[i][1] = bestStart[0][i];
  for (let t=2; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let i=t-1; i<n; i++) {
      let bestVal = -Infinity, bestL = -1;
      for (let l=t-1; l<=i; l++) {
        const prev = (l-1>=0) ? dp[l-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : -Infinity);
        const val = prev + bestStart[l][i];
        if (val > bestVal) { bestVal = val; bestL = l; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = bestVal;
      par[i][t] = bestL;
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

